window.CLATMOCK={"n":1,"title":"Mock Test 1","theme":"Core subjects","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"A balanced full-length paper across the core CLAT PG subjects: constitutional law, jurisprudence, criminal law, contract, torts, property, IPR, family law and more.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"p":[{"id":"P01","s":"Jurisprudence","sf":"Jurisprudence","t":"Hart's Concept of Law: Rules, Recognition and Obligation","x":"H.L.A. Hart's The Concept of Law (1961) begins by rejecting John Austin's account of law as the commands of a determinate sovereign, backed by threats and habitually obeyed. Hart argued that this model cannot explain power-conferring rules, such as those governing the making of wills or contracts, which command nothing yet are plainly law; nor can it explain the continuity of law when one sovereign is succeeded by another. In its place Hart offers the union of primary and secondary rules. Primary rules impose duties. Secondary rules are rules about rules: the rule of recognition identifies which rules count as law; rules of change allow rules to be made, altered or repealed; and rules of adjudication empower particular persons to determine authoritatively whether a rule has been broken. The rule of recognition is ultimate in the sense that its validity cannot be derived from any higher rule; its existence is a question of fact, namely whether officials in practice use it as the common standard for identifying law. Hart distinguishes the external from the internal point of view. An observer who merely records that people usually behave in a certain way takes the external view; a participant who treats the rule as a reason for her own conduct and a standard for criticising others takes the internal view. Officials must take the internal view towards the rule of recognition, while ordinary citizens may merely obey. Hart also separates 'being obliged' from 'having an obligation': a person who hands over money to a gunman is obliged to do so, but does not thereby have an obligation. In the Postscript, Hart accepted that a rule of recognition may incorporate moral criteria of validity ('inclusive' or 'soft' positivism), whereas Joseph Raz and other 'exclusive' positivists insist that the existence and content of law can always be identified without recourse to moral argument.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following was NOT identified by Hart as a deficiency in Austin's model?","o":["It cannot explain the continuity of law after a change of sovereign","It reduces all law to commands backed by threats","It fails to appreciate that the validity of law depends upon its moral content","It cannot account for rules that confer powers on private persons"],"a":2,"e":"Hart criticised Austin on power-conferring rules, continuity and the command-threat model. Austin was himself a positivist; Hart did not fault him for ignoring a moral test of validity.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The rule of recognition is described as 'ultimate' because","o":["it is the most morally defensible rule in the system","it is enforced by the strongest sanction","its validity is not derived from a higher rule and its existence rests on the practice of officials","it can be changed only by a rule of adjudication"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that the rule's validity cannot be derived from any higher rule and that its existence is a question of fact, namely official practice.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In State X, judges in different provinces apply different personal tests to decide which enactments are law, and each treats his test as a matter of private preference. Citizens, however, obey most enactments. On Hart's theory, which conclusion is most accurate?","o":["A legal system exists only if the rules are morally just","A legal system exists because citizens generally obey","A legal system exists because primary rules are in force","A legal system is doubtful because officials do not share a common standard of recognition"],"a":3,"e":"Citizen obedience alone is insufficient. Officials must collectively accept a common rule of recognition as a public standard; its absence defeats the existence of a legal system.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best illustration of the 'internal point of view'?","o":["A foreign lawyer who predicts what a court will do","A citizen who pays tax only because he fears prosecution","A judge who treats a statute as a reason for her decision and criticises a colleague who departs from it","A sociologist who counts how many motorists stop at red lights"],"a":2,"e":"The internal view involves treating the rule as a reason for action and a standard of criticism. The other options reflect the external view or mere obedience.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Country Z's constitution declares that any statute violating equal human dignity is invalid. Which statement best captures the positivist debate on this provision?","o":["Exclusive positivists accept the provision as a criterion of validity but inclusive positivists reject it","Both inclusive and exclusive positivists must deny that this is law","Inclusive positivists may accept that the rule of recognition itself incorporates a moral criterion of validity, while exclusive positivists deny that a moral test can be a condition of legal validity","Neither school can recognise a written constitution"],"a":2,"e":"Per the passage, soft positivism allows moral criteria to be part of the rule of recognition; Raz's exclusive positivism requires law's existence and content to be identifiable without moral argument.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A shopkeeper pays 'protection money' to a local gang solely out of fear of violence. Applying the distinction drawn in the passage, he is","o":["obliged to pay, but does not thereby have an obligation","under a legal obligation to pay","both obliged and under an obligation","neither obliged nor under an obligation"],"a":0,"e":"The gunman example shows that coercion creates being obliged; having an obligation involves acceptance of a rule as a standard.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P02","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governors, Bills and the Limits of Judicial Timelines","x":"Article 200 of the Constitution provides that when a Bill passed by a State Legislature is presented to the Governor, the Governor may assent to it, withhold assent, or reserve it for the consideration of the President. The first proviso adds that, except for a Money Bill, the Governor may return the Bill as soon as possible with a message requesting reconsideration; if the House passes it again, with or without amendments, the Governor shall not withhold assent. The Article prescribes no time-limit, which led to prolonged inaction by Governors in several States. In April 2025, in State of Tamil Nadu v. Governor of Tamil Nadu, a two-judge bench held that the Governor's refusal to act on re-passed Bills was illegal, read timelines into the Article (generally one to three months, depending on the course adopted) and, invoking Article 142, declared ten Bills to have been 'deemed' assented to. The President then made a reference under Article 143, asking whether courts can impose timelines or deem assent. In November 2025, a Constitution Bench of five judges delivered its advisory opinion. It held that the Governor has three constitutionally available courses: grant assent, reserve the Bill for the President, or withhold assent and return a non-money Bill with a message; the Governor cannot simply sit on a Bill indefinitely, because that would reduce the elected legislature to a supplicant and permit an informal 'pocket veto'. At the same time, the Bench held that courts cannot prescribe rigid timelines where the Constitution has chosen to prescribe none, nor deem a Bill to be assented to, since doing so would substitute the judiciary for a constitutional functionary. Article 142, it said, cannot be used to supplant the Constitution's allocation of powers. It nevertheless accepted that where inaction is prolonged, unexplained and indefinite, a court may issue a limited direction that the Governor decide within a reasonable time, without commenting on the merits of the decision.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a course open to the Governor under Article 200, as explained in the passage?","o":["Granting assent to the Bill","Reserving the Bill for the consideration of the President","Withholding assent and retaining the Bill indefinitely without any communication","Withholding assent to a non-money Bill and returning it with a message"],"a":2,"e":"The Bench held that indefinite retention amounts to an impermissible pocket veto; only the three courses listed are available.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"According to the November 2025 opinion, the concept of 'deemed assent' is","o":["valid only for Money Bills","permissible if the delay exceeds three months","valid, since Article 142 empowers the Supreme Court to do complete justice","impermissible, because it substitutes the judiciary for a constitutional functionary"],"a":3,"e":"The Bench held courts cannot deem assent, and Article 142 cannot supplant the constitutional allocation of powers.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Why, according to the passage, did the Constitution Bench decline to fix rigid timelines?","o":["Because the Constitution deliberately prescribes no time-limit and fixing one would amount to rewriting the text","Because timelines apply only to the President","Because the State Legislature can always re-pass a Bill","Because Governors enjoy absolute immunity under Article 361"],"a":0,"e":"The Bench reasoned that courts cannot impose rigid timelines where the Constitution has chosen to prescribe none.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A Governor has taken no decision on a non-money Bill for more than two years and offers no reason. Consistent with the opinion, the appropriate judicial response is","o":["to declare that the Bill has become law","to dismiss any petition as non-justiciable in all circumstances","to assent to the Bill on the Governor's behalf","to direct the Governor to decide within a reasonable time without examining the merits of the decision"],"a":3,"e":"Prolonged, unexplained and indefinite inaction permits a limited direction to decide within a reasonable time; merits remain outside review.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In the passage, the expression 'pocket veto' refers to","o":["the President's power to return a Bill for reconsideration","the effective defeat of a Bill by keeping it pending indefinitely without a decision","the Governor's power to reserve a Bill for the President","the legislature's power to re-pass a Bill"],"a":1,"e":"The Bench used the phrase for informal defeat of a Bill through unlimited inaction.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which statement about the Article 143 reference is most accurate on the basis of the passage and the Constitution?","o":["The reference can be made only by a Governor","The Court is bound to answer every question and its opinion operates as a judgment inter partes","The reference can be used to reverse an earlier judgment between the same parties","The reference seeks the Court's advisory opinion on questions of law or fact of public importance, and is not a decision in a dispute between parties"],"a":3,"e":"Article 143 is advisory jurisdiction; the Court gives an opinion on a question referred by the President and does not adjudicate a lis between parties.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P03","s":"Criminal Law","sf":"Criminal Law","t":"The New Criminal Codes: BNS, BNSS and BSA","x":"The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (BNS), the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (BNSS) and the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 (BSA) came into force on 1 July 2024, replacing the Indian Penal Code, the Code of Criminal Procedure and the Evidence Act respectively. The BNS does not retain the offence of sedition; instead Section 152 penalises acts which endanger the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India, a formulation critics argue is as broad as the old provision. It introduces organised crime (Section 111) and terrorist acts (Section 113) as offences in the general penal code, recognises community service as a punishment for certain petty offences, and criminalises sexual intercourse obtained by deceitful means, including a false promise of marriage (Section 69). Murder committed by a group of five or more persons acting in concert on the ground of race, caste, community, sex, place of birth, language or personal belief attracts death or imprisonment for life (Section 103(2)). The BNSS permits 'Zero FIR', allowing information about a cognisable offence to be registered at any police station irrespective of territorial jurisdiction and then transferred. It also provides for electronic filing of information, which must be signed by the informant within three days. Where an investigation cannot be completed in the usual period, detention before the charge-sheet is limited to sixty days, or ninety days for offences punishable with death, life imprisonment or imprisonment of ten years or more; police custody of up to fifteen days may be authorised in whole or in part during the first forty or sixty days of that period, respectively. The BNSS also allows trial of a proclaimed offender in absentia, after charges are framed and a waiting period of ninety days has elapsed. Under the BSA, electronic records are treated as documents and may be admitted as primary evidence, but a certificate in the prescribed form, signed by the person in charge of the device and an expert, is required.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following is correctly stated on the basis of the passage?","o":["Sedition has been retained in the BNS in an unchanged form","Section 152 of the BNS penalises acts endangering the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India","Organised crime continues to be dealt with only under special statutes","Community service is a punishment for all offences under the BNS"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that Section 152 replaces sedition with an offence based on endangering sovereignty, unity and integrity; the other options are contrary to the text.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"An accused is arrested for an offence punishable with imprisonment for life. Police custody may be authorised","o":["for up to fifteen days, in whole or in part, during the first sixty days of detention","for an unlimited period if the Magistrate is satisfied","for up to fifteen days, only in the first forty days of detention","only after the charge-sheet is filed"],"a":0,"e":"For offences punishable with death, life or ten years or more, the period is ninety days and police custody may be authorised in the first sixty days.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is a condition for trial in absentia under the BNSS, as described in the passage?","o":["The accused must have been convicted earlier","The trial may begin before charges are framed","The informant must have consented in writing","The accused must be a proclaimed offender and the trial may proceed after charges are framed and ninety days have elapsed"],"a":3,"e":"Trial in absentia is available against a proclaimed offender after charges are framed and a waiting period of ninety days.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A resident of Delhi is robbed in Jaipur and reports the matter at a Delhi police station. Under the 'Zero FIR' mechanism","o":["the Delhi police must investigate and cannot transfer the case","the information may be registered at the Delhi station and then transferred to the station having jurisdiction","the information may be registered only if the Magistrate permits","the police must refuse as Delhi has no territorial jurisdiction"],"a":1,"e":"Zero FIR permits registration irrespective of territorial jurisdiction, followed by transfer.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A prosecutor wishes to rely on a copy of CCTV footage stored on a hard drive. On the basis of the passage, which statement is most accurate?","o":["The footage may be admitted as primary evidence, but a certificate in the prescribed form signed by the person in charge of the device and an expert is required","The footage is inadmissible because it is not paper","The footage is admissible only as secondary evidence of no value","No certificate is needed if a police officer testifies"],"a":0,"e":"The BSA treats electronic records as documents but requires the prescribed certificate by the person in charge and an expert.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Three persons beat a man to death solely because of his caste. Which provision is most appropriately attracted on the facts?","o":["Section 103(1), the general offence of murder, because Section 103(2) requires a group of five or more","Section 113, because it is a terrorist act","Section 103(2), because the killing was caste-based","Section 111, organised crime, because more than one person is involved"],"a":0,"e":"The caste-based group-murder provision requires five or more persons acting in concert. With three, liability falls under the general murder provision.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P04","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law / Information Technology","t":"Data Protection and the Right to Privacy","x":"In K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017), a nine-judge Bench held that privacy is a fundamental right under Articles 14, 19 and 21. A restriction on privacy must satisfy a test of proportionality: it must be backed by law, pursue a legitimate State aim, bear a rational connection to that aim and be necessary in the sense that no less intrusive alternative exists, and be accompanied by procedural safeguards against abuse. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is India's first horizontal data protection statute. It speaks of the Data Principal, the individual to whom the data relates, and the Data Fiduciary, which determines the purpose and means of processing. Processing is lawful principally on the basis of consent, which must be free, specific, informed, unconditional and unambiguous, though the Act also lists 'legitimate uses' such as compliance with law and medical emergencies where consent is not required. For a child, defined as a person below eighteen years, verifiable parental consent is required, and the Act bars tracking, behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising directed at children. The Data Protection Board of India adjudicates breaches, and penalties may extend to Rs 250 crore for failure to take reasonable security safeguards. Critics point to Section 17, under which the Central Government may exempt State instrumentalities from the Act by notification in the interests of matters such as sovereignty, security and public order, and to Section 44(3), which amends Section 8(1)(j) of the Right to Information Act, 2005 so that personal information may be refused without the earlier balancing of the public interest. The Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025 were notified in November 2025 and are being brought into force in phases: the provisions relating to the Board take effect at once, those on Consent Managers after twelve months and the core obligations of Data Fiduciaries after eighteen months.","q":[{"q":"An online pharmacy decides what patient data to collect and for what purpose. In the vocabulary of the Act, the pharmacy and the patient are respectively","o":["Data Fiduciary and Consent Manager","Data Fiduciary and Data Principal","Data Principal and Data Fiduciary","Consent Manager and Data Principal"],"a":1,"e":"The entity determining purpose and means is the Data Fiduciary; the individual to whom data relates is the Data Principal.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A gaming app uses a fifteen-year-old's browsing history, with her parent's consent, to show her targeted advertisements. Under the Act as described, this is","o":["permissible only if the Board is informed","lawful, because she is above thirteen","impermissible, because targeted advertising directed at children is barred","lawful, because parental consent was obtained"],"a":2,"e":"The Act bars targeted advertising and behavioural monitoring directed at persons below eighteen; parental consent does not cure the bar.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an element of the proportionality test as stated in the passage?","o":["Existence of a law","A legitimate State aim","Necessity, with no less intrusive alternative","Prior approval of Parliament for every individual restriction"],"a":3,"e":"The passage lists legality, legitimate aim, rational connection and necessity, and procedural safeguards; individual parliamentary approval is not required.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The criticism directed at Section 44(3) of the Act is that it","o":["repeals Section 8 of the RTI Act altogether","makes all personal information public","removes the balancing of public interest when refusing disclosure of personal information","extends the Right to Information Act to private companies"],"a":2,"e":"The amendment to Section 8(1)(j) allows refusal of personal information without the earlier public-interest balancing.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A Data Fiduciary fails to adopt reasonable security safeguards and a breach occurs. The maximum penalty indicated in the passage is","o":["Rs 250 crore","Rs 50 crore","Rs 100 crore","Rs 500 crore"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states penalties may extend to Rs 250 crore for this failure.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Fourteen months after notification of the Rules, which of the following has most likely taken effect?","o":["None, as the Rules are yet to commence","Only the provisions relating to the Board","All provisions, including core obligations","The provisions relating to the Board and to Consent Managers, but not yet the core obligations of Data Fiduciaries"],"a":3,"e":"Board provisions were immediate and Consent Manager provisions apply after twelve months; core obligations follow after eighteen months.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P05","s":"Jurisprudence","sf":"Jurisprudence","t":"Dworkin's Principles and Fuller's Inner Morality of Law","x":"Ronald Dworkin attacked Hart's positivism on the ground that law consists not only of rules but also of principles, standards that judges are obliged to consider because they express a requirement of justice, fairness or morality. In Riggs v. Palmer, a New York court refused to let an heir who had murdered his grandfather take under the will, invoking the principle that no one should profit from his own wrong, though the statute of wills contained no such exception. Dworkin argued that rules apply in an all-or-nothing fashion, whereas principles have a dimension of weight and may be outweighed by other principles without ceasing to be valid. Because principles are not validated by their pedigree, that is, by enactment in accordance with a rule of recognition, Dworkin claimed that positivism cannot explain how judges decide hard cases. He proposed that law is a matter of integrity: judges should decide hard cases by constructing the interpretation that best fits and justifies the existing legal materials, an ideal embodied in his imagined judge Hercules, and he defended the 'right answer thesis'. Lon Fuller, by contrast, approached law through its 'inner morality', eight desiderata without which the enterprise of subjecting human conduct to rules fails: generality; promulgation; prospectivity; clarity; consistency; possibility of compliance; constancy through time; and congruence between declared rules and official action. Fuller argued that a total failure in any of these does not produce a bad legal system but no legal system at all. In the celebrated debate arising from the post-war 'grudge informer' case, Hart maintained that a wicked enactment may nonetheless be valid law, though it may be morally right to disobey it, whereas Fuller contended that fidelity to law could not be separated from the moral qualities of legality.","q":[{"q":"According to Dworkin, the essential difference between rules and principles is that","o":["rules are applied only by Parliament while principles are applied by judges","rules apply in an all-or-nothing way while principles have weight and may be outweighed","rules are moral while principles are legal","principles are enacted while rules are customary"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that rules apply all-or-nothing whereas principles have a dimension of weight.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Riggs v. Palmer is used by Dworkin to show that","o":["a will can never be revoked","judges may disregard statutes at will","courts must follow the literal text of statutes","legal systems contain principles that influence decisions even though no rule of recognition validated them by pedigree"],"a":3,"e":"The court invoked a principle not found in the statute, illustrating Dworkin's point about principles beyond pedigree.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A Government publishes an order only in a sealed file known to officials and punishes citizens for breach of its terms. Which of Fuller's desiderata is most directly violated?","o":["Promulgation","Possibility of compliance","Constancy through time","Prospectivity"],"a":0,"e":"Secret rules which citizens cannot know offend the requirement of promulgation.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A statute requires every taxpayer to file returns within 24 hours of the close of the financial year, a task that is physically impossible for most. Which desideratum is chiefly offended?","o":["Generality","Possibility of compliance","Congruence","Clarity"],"a":1,"e":"Rules requiring the impossible violate the requirement that compliance be possible.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which statement best represents the difference between Hart and Fuller in the 'grudge informer' debate?","o":["Hart held that a wicked enactment may be valid law but disobeyed; Fuller held that legality has inherent moral qualities","Both held that the enactment was not law","Hart denied that the enactment was a statute; Fuller accepted it as law","Both held that the enactment was binding on judges without exception"],"a":0,"e":"Hart separated validity from morality; Fuller insisted that legality carries an inner morality.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following would Dworkin most likely accept?","o":["Judges in hard cases exercise unfettered discretion, in which they legislate","Judges should be bound by a rigid mechanical application of precedents","In hard cases, judges should seek the interpretation that best fits and morally justifies the existing legal materials","The identification of law depends solely on social facts of pedigree"],"a":2,"e":"This is law as integrity, in contrast to the positivist discretion thesis.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P06","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Sub-classification of Scheduled Castes and the Creamy Layer","x":"Article 341 empowers the President to specify the castes to be deemed Scheduled Castes in each State, and Articles 15(4) and 16(4) enable the State to make special provisions and reservations for backward classes inadequately represented in services. In E.V. Chinnaiah v. State of Andhra Pradesh (2004), a five-judge Bench held that Scheduled Castes form a homogeneous group and that sub-classifying them for reservation would amount to tampering with the Presidential List. In State of Punjab v. Davinder Singh (August 2024), a seven-judge Bench, by a majority of six to one, overruled Chinnaiah. The majority reasoned that Article 341 merely identifies castes for the purpose of deeming them Scheduled Castes and does not prevent the State from differentiating among them for the purposes of reservation, and that historical and empirical evidence shows that some Scheduled Castes are more disadvantaged than others. The Court held, however, that sub-classification must not be arbitrary. The State must justify it by 'quantifiable and demonstrable data' showing the inadequate representation of the sub-class in services, rather than by political expediency or mere assumption; the State cannot reserve the entire quota for a single sub-class, and its decisions remain open to judicial review. In separate opinions, some judges, including Justice B.R. Gavai, suggested that the 'creamy layer' principle, which in Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992) was applied to Other Backward Classes, should also be applied to identify and exclude the advanced among Scheduled Castes and Tribes; this suggestion was not part of the operative holding of the Bench. The dissenting judge held that the Presidential List is a closed one, and that any alteration of it lies exclusively within the power of Parliament under Article 341(2).","q":[{"q":"The decision in State of Punjab v. Davinder Singh overruled which earlier decision?","o":["Indra Sawhney v. Union of India","Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala","M. Nagaraj v. Union of India","E.V. Chinnaiah v. State of Andhra Pradesh"],"a":3,"e":"The passage records that the seven-judge Bench overruled Chinnaiah (2004).","d":"Easy"},{"q":"According to the majority, a State seeking to sub-classify Scheduled Castes must","o":["apply the creamy-layer test in all cases","obtain the prior consent of Parliament","reserve the entire quota for the most backward sub-class","rely on quantifiable and demonstrable data showing inadequate representation of the sub-class"],"a":3,"e":"The majority insisted on empirical justification and rejected political expediency.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A State reserves the whole Scheduled Caste quota in a recruitment for one sub-caste, without data. On the passage, this action is","o":["valid if the sub-caste is the most numerous","open to invalidation, as the State cannot reserve the entire quota for one sub-class and must rely on data","invalid only if Parliament amends Article 341","valid as sub-classification is now allowed"],"a":1,"e":"Both limits stated in the passage are violated: no data and exclusive reservation.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The majority's reasoning on Article 341 is that the Article","o":["merely identifies castes to be deemed Scheduled Castes and does not prohibit differentiated treatment for reservation","gives States power to modify the list","freezes the Presidential List and precludes any State action","applies only to Scheduled Tribes"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records this reasoning in explaining why Chinnaiah was overruled.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The status of the creamy-layer suggestion for Scheduled Castes in the judgment is best described as","o":["an overruling of Indra Sawhney","an observation in separate opinions, not part of the operative holding","a binding ratio of the seven-judge Bench","the unanimous view of all seven judges"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that the suggestion was not part of the operative holding.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following would the dissenting judge most likely agree with?","o":["Chinnaiah was wrongly decided","States may freely sub-classify without data","The creamy layer must be applied to every reserved category","Only Parliament can alter the Presidential List under Article 341(2), and sub-classification by States amounts to such an alteration"],"a":3,"e":"The dissent held the list to be closed and alterable only by Parliament.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P07","s":"Criminal Procedure","sf":"Criminal Procedure / Constitutional Law","t":"Grounds of Arrest, Bail and Special Statutes","x":"Article 22(1) of the Constitution provides that no person who is arrested shall be detained in custody without being informed, as soon as may be, of the grounds for such arrest, nor be denied the right to consult and be defended by a legal practitioner of his choice. In Pankaj Bansal v. Union of India (2023), the Supreme Court held that the Enforcement Directorate must furnish the written grounds of arrest to the arrested person under the Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002 (PMLA), and that merely reading them out is insufficient. In Prabir Purkayastha v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2024), the same requirement was applied to arrests under the Unlawful Activities (Prevention) Act, 1967. In Mihir Rajesh Shah v. State of Maharashtra (2025), the Court held that the requirement applies to arrests under all penal statutes, that the grounds must be communicated in writing in a language the arrestee understands, and that non-compliance renders the arrest illegal and vitiates the subsequent remand. Section 45 of the PMLA imposes 'twin conditions' for bail: the court must be satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for believing that the accused is not guilty and is not likely to commit any offence while on bail. In Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb (2021) and in Manish Sisodia v. Directorate of Enforcement (2024), the Court has held that prolonged incarceration without trial offends Article 21, and that the principle 'bail is the rule and jail the exception' applies even to special statutes with stringent conditions. Constitutional courts may therefore grant bail notwithstanding statutory restrictions where the trial is delayed and the accused has suffered long incarceration.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following is consistent with the Supreme Court's decision in Pankaj Bansal?","o":["The Enforcement Directorate must give the written grounds of arrest to the arrested person","The grounds need be given only to the arrestee's relatives","Oral communication of the grounds of arrest is always sufficient","The requirement applies only to arrests by the police"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that written grounds must be furnished and mere reading out is insufficient.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Mihir Rajesh Shah, the Court held that failure to supply the grounds of arrest in writing","o":["entitles the accused to compensation only","is a mere irregularity","has no bearing on the remand order","renders the arrest illegal and vitiates the remand"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that non-compliance renders the arrest illegal and vitiates the remand.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A person arrested for an ordinary offence under the BNS is told the grounds orally and is not given any written grounds. According to the passage, the arrest is","o":["valid because the requirement applies only to UAPA arrests","valid if the arrestee is represented by a lawyer","open to challenge as illegal, since the written grounds requirement applies to all penal statutes","valid because the requirement applies only to PMLA arrests"],"a":2,"e":"Mihir Rajesh Shah extended the requirement to all penal statutes.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The 'twin conditions' under Section 45 of the PMLA require the court to be satisfied that","o":["the Enforcement Directorate has consented","the accused has been in custody for ninety days","there are reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty and is not likely to commit any offence on bail","the accused has furnished a surety and a bond"],"a":2,"e":"This is the text of the twin conditions as given in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"What is the effect of Najeeb and Sisodia on the stringent bail provisions of special statutes?","o":["Only the High Court can grant bail under special statutes","The bail conditions in special statutes are struck down","Prolonged incarceration without trial may justify bail despite statutory restrictions, as Article 21 prevails","Special statutes override Article 21"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that constitutional courts may grant bail despite restrictions where the trial is delayed.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following inferences is most strongly supported by the passage?","o":["Article 22(1) and Article 21 give the courts a basis to scrutinise both the manner of arrest and the length of incarceration under stringent statutes","Bail can never be granted in special statutes","The PMLA is unconstitutional","Statutory restrictions on bail operate in isolation from constitutional guarantees"],"a":0,"e":"The decisions rely on Articles 22(1) and 21 to control arrest procedure and prolonged detention.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P08","s":"Environmental Law","sf":"Environmental Law / International Law","t":"Climate Change: From the Great Indian Bustard to The Hague","x":"In M.K. Ranjitsinh v. Union of India (2024), the Supreme Court, while considering the protection of the Great Indian Bustard from overhead power lines, held that the right to life under Article 21 and the right to equality under Article 14 include a right to be free from the adverse effects of climate change. The Court reasoned that without a clean environment stable in climate, the right to health and life cannot be fully realised, and that the State must balance the transition to renewable energy with the protection of endangered species. On 23 July 2025, the International Court of Justice delivered a unanimous advisory opinion on the Obligations of States in respect of Climate Change, in response to a request by the General Assembly prompted by Vanuatu and other small island States. The Court held that States are bound by the climate treaties, including the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change and the Paris Agreement, as well as by customary international law, which includes the duty to prevent significant harm to the environment and to cooperate. It held that the standard of due diligence is stringent, in view of the grave risk, and that the 1.5 degree Celsius temperature goal is the primary target under the Paris Agreement. National determined contributions must, collectively and individually, reflect the highest possible ambition. The Court added that the production and consumption of fossil fuels, the grant of exploration licences and fossil fuel subsidies may constitute an internationally wrongful act attributable to the State, and that such a breach entails legal consequences, including cessation, guarantees of non-repetition and full reparation, in the form of restitution, compensation or satisfaction. Although advisory opinions are not binding, they carry legal authority and are likely to be cited before national courts and in contentious proceedings.","q":[{"q":"The Supreme Court in M.K. Ranjitsinh recognised the right against adverse effects of climate change as part of","o":["the Directive Principles alone","Article 19(1)(g) only","Articles 14 and 21","Article 32 only"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that Articles 14 and 21 include this right.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"According to the ICJ opinion described, the standard of due diligence applicable to States is","o":["stringent, in view of the grave risk to the climate system","minimal, as climate is a global matter","applicable only to developed States","dependent only on the State's wishes"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that the due diligence standard is stringent.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT stated as a possible legal consequence of an internationally wrongful act in the passage?","o":["Full reparation","Automatic expulsion from the United Nations","Cessation","Guarantees of non-repetition"],"a":1,"e":"Cessation, non-repetition and reparation are listed; expulsion from the UN is not.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The legal status of the ICJ's advisory opinion is best described as","o":["binding on all States as a judgment","not binding in itself, but carrying legal authority and likely to be cited","binding only on Vanuatu","of no value"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states advisory opinions are not binding but carry authority.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A State grants fresh licences for coal exploration, despite its commitments under the Paris Agreement. On the passage, the ICJ's reasoning suggests that this","o":["is a purely domestic matter","may constitute an internationally wrongful act attributable to the State","is relevant only for contentious cases between neighbours","is permitted as long as the State submits its NDC"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the grant of exploration licences may constitute an internationally wrongful act.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best inference connecting the two decisions?","o":["Both treat climate protection as a matter of moral exhortation, without legal content","Both are advisory opinions","Both hold that renewable energy has priority over all other interests","Both treat climate protection as carrying binding legal obligations, one grounded in fundamental rights at the national level and the other in treaty and customary duties at the international level"],"a":3,"e":"The Supreme Court tied it to Articles 14 and 21; the ICJ tied it to treaty and customary law.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P09","s":"Arbitration Law","sf":"Arbitration Law","t":"Arbitration: Non-signatories, Stamping and Limits of Court Intervention","x":"The Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, as amended in 2015 and 2019, seeks to limit judicial intervention in arbitral proceedings. Section 34 allows a party to apply to set aside an award on narrow grounds, including incapacity, invalid agreement, want of notice, an award beyond the scope of the submission, and conflict with the public policy of India, which now includes fraud, corruption, violation of fundamental policy of Indian law and conflict with the most basic notions of morality or justice. An award in a domestic arbitration may also be set aside for 'patent illegality appearing on the face of the award', though not by mere erroneous application of law or re-appreciation of evidence. In Gayatri Balasamy v. ISG Novasoft Technologies (2025), a Constitution Bench held by a majority of four to one that although Section 34 confers on courts the power to set aside, and not to modify, an award, a limited power of modification exists: courts may correct clerical, computational or typographical errors, sever the invalid portion from the valid portion of an award and modify post-award interest; the Supreme Court may, in rare cases, use Article 142 but only with great caution. In Cox and Kings Ltd v. SAP India Pvt Ltd (2023), a Constitution Bench held that the 'group of companies' doctrine can be applied to bind non-signatory affiliates to an arbitration agreement, where there is a mutual intention of the parties to do so, to be gathered from factors such as the conduct of the parties and the commonality of the subject matter. In In Re: Interplay between Arbitration Agreements under the Arbitration Act and the Stamp Act (2023), a seven-judge Bench held that an unstamped arbitration agreement is not void or void ab initio, but inadmissible in evidence, a defect curable by payment of stamp duty. Courts at the referral stage under Section 11 should confine themselves to examining the existence of an arbitration agreement on a prima facie basis, leaving other questions to the tribunal under the principle of competence-competence.","q":[{"q":"Under the passage, which of the following is NOT a power of a court under Section 34, as clarified in Gayatri Balasamy?","o":["To sever the invalid portion from the valid portion of an award","To set aside an award on the statutory grounds","To re-appreciate the evidence and substitute its own view on merits","To correct computational or typographical errors in the award"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that re-appreciation of evidence is not permitted; the other options are within the limited power.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A non-signatory affiliate of a signatory company is sought to be bound to the arbitration clause. Following Cox and Kings, the decisive test is","o":["whether there was a mutual intention to bind the non-signatory, gathered from conduct and the commonality of the subject matter","whether the main contract has been stamped","whether the affiliate holds shares in the signatory","whether the affiliate is part of the same group, irrespective of intent"],"a":0,"e":"The doctrine applies only where mutual intention exists.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Seven-Judge Bench held that an unstamped arbitration agreement is","o":["enforceable only before the High Court","void ab initio","valid and admissible without any duty","inadmissible in evidence but curable by payment of duty"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states it is not void, but inadmissible, a curable defect.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"At the Section 11 stage, the court is expected to","o":["decide the validity of the main contract","examine the existence of the arbitration agreement on a prima facie basis","decide the merits of the dispute","examine whether the claim is time-barred in all cases"],"a":1,"e":"The referral court's role is limited under competence-competence.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"An award contains a clear arithmetical error in computing interest and also awards a sum in respect of a matter not submitted to arbitration, severable from the rest. Based on Gayatri Balasamy, the court may","o":["do nothing, as the court has no power to modify","re-write the award to the court's own assessment","correct the computational error and sever the portion beyond the submission, leaving the remainder intact","set aside the entire award in all cases"],"a":2,"e":"Correction of computational errors and severance are within the limited power recognised.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The overall policy that emerges from the passage is best described as","o":["preference of litigation over arbitration","maximum court supervision over arbitration","minimal and carefully calibrated court intervention, respecting the tribunal's competence","complete exclusion of court involvement"],"a":2,"e":"All the decisions indicate limited intervention and respect for the tribunal.","d":"Easy"}]},{"id":"P10","s":"Law of Contract","sf":"Law of Contract","t":"Free Consent, Restraint of Trade and Liquidated Damages","x":"Under Section 10 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, an agreement is a contract if made by the free consent of competent parties for a lawful consideration and object, and not expressly declared void. Under Section 14, consent is not free when caused by coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation or mistake. Where consent is caused by coercion, undue influence, fraud or misrepresentation, the contract is voidable at the option of the party whose consent was so caused. Under Section 16, a contract is induced by undue influence where the relations between the parties are such that one is in a position to dominate the will of the other and uses that position to obtain an unfair advantage. The courts have recognised, as in Central Inland Water Transport Corporation v. Brojo Nath Ganguly (1986), that unconscionable contracts, where bargaining power is grossly unequal, may be struck down as contrary to public policy under Section 23. Section 27 declares void every agreement by which anyone is restrained from exercising a lawful profession, trade or business, with a narrow exception for the sale of the goodwill of a business. In Niranjan Shankar Golikari v. Century Spinning (1967), the Supreme Court distinguished between a covenant operative during the term of employment, which is valid, and one operative after the termination of the contract, which, if in restraint of trade, is void under Section 27. On breach, Section 73 awards compensation for loss that naturally arose in the usual course of things or that the parties knew likely to result. Section 74 provides that where a sum is named as payable upon breach, the party complaining is entitled to receive 'reasonable compensation' not exceeding the amount so named, whether or not actual damage is proved, though the Supreme Court in Kailash Nath Associates v. DDA (2015) held that the party must still show that some loss was caused, except where loss is impossible to prove.","q":[{"q":"An agreement obtained by undue influence is, under the passage,","o":["valid and binding","illegal and punishable","voidable at the option of the party whose consent was so caused","void ab initio"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states such a contract is voidable at that party's option.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"An employee in a bank agrees, in his contract, not to work for any competitor for five years after he leaves. On the principle in Golikari, this post-termination covenant is","o":["void, to the extent it is in restraint of trade under Section 27","valid, since it is part of the contract","valid only if the employer pays compensation","valid if the employee is highly paid"],"a":0,"e":"Post-termination restraints are void under Section 27; only restraints during employment are valid.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the exception to Section 27 mentioned in the passage?","o":["Sale of goodwill of a business","Agency contracts","Partnership between two firms","Employment contracts"],"a":0,"e":"The passage identifies the sale of goodwill as the narrow exception.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A contract of loan names Rs 5 lakh as payable upon breach. The lender proves no loss at all, though loss was capable of proof. Following Kailash Nath, the lender","o":["may recover double the sum","is entitled to nothing in any event","is entitled to reasonable compensation not exceeding Rs 5 lakh only on showing that some loss was caused","is entitled to Rs 5 lakh automatically"],"a":2,"e":"Section 74 requires some loss to be shown, unless proof is impossible.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The Brojo Nath Ganguly decision illustrates that","o":["unconscionable contracts arising from grossly unequal bargaining power can be struck down as against public policy","freedom of contract is absolute","courts have no power to interfere with standard-form contracts","only legislation can regulate contract terms"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that such contracts may be struck down under Section 23.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a vitiating factor of free consent under Section 14 as set out in the passage?","o":["Mistake","Inadequacy of consideration","Coercion","Misrepresentation"],"a":1,"e":"Inadequacy of consideration is not a ground in Section 14.","d":"Easy"}]},{"id":"P11","s":"Law of Torts","sf":"Law of Torts","t":"Strict and Absolute Liability, and Professional Negligence","x":"Under the rule in Rylands v. Fletcher (1868), a person who for his own purposes brings and keeps on his land something likely to do mischief if it escapes is liable, without proof of negligence, for the damage that is the natural consequence of its escape. The rule is subject to exceptions, including an act of God, the act of a stranger, the plaintiff's consent or default, and statutory authority. After the Oleum gas leak in Delhi, the Supreme Court in M.C. Mehta v. Union of India (1987) declined to follow the exceptions to the rule and evolved the principle of absolute liability for enterprises engaged in hazardous or inherently dangerous activity: such an enterprise is absolutely liable to compensate all those affected by an accident, and none of the exceptions applies. The Court added that the quantum of compensation must be correlated to the magnitude and capacity of the enterprise, so that it has a deterrent effect. The Public Liability Insurance Act, 1991 imposes no-fault liability for hazardous substances, and the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 empowers the Tribunal to award relief and compensation in environmental cases. In the context of negligence, the test in Donoghue v. Stevenson asks whether the defendant owed a duty of care to a neighbour, one who is closely and directly affected by his acts. In professional negligence, the Bolam test asks whether the professional acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of professionals. For criminal liability of doctors, the Supreme Court in Jacob Mathew v. State of Punjab (2005) held that the negligence must be 'gross', beyond mere lack of care, and that a private complaint should not be entertained unless supported by the credible opinion of another competent doctor.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following exceptions, available under Rylands v. Fletcher, does NOT apply to an enterprise under the rule of absolute liability?","o":["Act of God","Default of the plaintiff","Act of a stranger","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that none of the exceptions applies under absolute liability.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Supreme Court's reasoning on the quantum of compensation in M.C. Mehta was that it should","o":["be fixed by the enterprise","be capped at the value of the plaintiff's property","be nominal","be proportional to the magnitude and capacity of the enterprise, with a deterrent effect"],"a":3,"e":"The Court correlated compensation with the enterprise's capacity.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A chemical factory stores toxic gas which escapes due to sabotage by an unknown third party, injuring neighbours. Under the rule of absolute liability as described, the factory is","o":["not liable, since the act of a stranger is a defence","liable only if it was negligent","liable, since no exception applies to a hazardous enterprise","liable only to the extent of the insurance cover"],"a":2,"e":"Under absolute liability, no exception, including that of a stranger, applies.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Bolam test is concerned with whether the professional","o":["obtained the patient's written consent","acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of professionals","took the highest possible care","charged reasonable fees"],"a":1,"e":"This is the Bolam test as set out in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A patient files a private criminal complaint against a surgeon for negligence without any medical opinion. In line with Jacob Mathew, the complaint","o":["should not ordinarily be entertained without the credible opinion of another competent doctor","must be entertained as of right","must be transferred to a civil court","must be treated as a case of murder"],"a":0,"e":"Jacob Mathew requires a prima facie opinion of another competent doctor.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following statements best states the difference between strict liability and absolute liability as derived from the passage?","o":["Strict liability has exceptions and absolute liability has none","Absolute liability is limited to the value of the property","There is no difference","Strict liability applies only to enterprises"],"a":0,"e":"The passage notes that the Court refused to follow the exceptions to Rylands v. Fletcher.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P12","s":"Intellectual Property Law","sf":"Intellectual Property Law","t":"Artificial Intelligence, Authorship and Patentability","x":"Section 13 of the Copyright Act, 1957 confers copyright on original literary, dramatic, musical and artistic works. Section 2(d)(vi) provides that, in relation to any literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work which is computer-generated, the 'author' is the person who causes the work to be created. The Supreme Court in Eastern Book Company v. D.B. Modak (2008) rejected both the 'sweat of the brow' and the 'creativity' extremes, and adopted the 'modicum of creativity' standard, holding that a work must involve some intellectual effort and skill, more than mere labour, to qualify as original. The rise of generative artificial intelligence has presented two questions to the courts and legislators: whether output generated by an AI model is protectable, and whether the use of copyrighted material to train such models infringes the copyright. In Asian News International v. OpenAI, pending before the Delhi High Court, the news agency alleges that its articles were used without licence to train a large language model and reproduced in its outputs; the defendant claims that the training involves non-expressive processing and is not infringement, and questions arise on the application of the closed list of exceptions in Section 52, which includes fair dealing for private use, research, criticism, review and reporting of current events. Separately, Section 3(k) of the Patents Act, 1970 excludes from patentability a 'computer programme per se', and mathematical or business methods. In Ferid Allani v. Union of India (2019), the Delhi High Court held that computer-related inventions which demonstrate a 'technical effect' or 'technical contribution' are not excluded merely because they involve a computer programme. Several High Courts have also granted personality-rights injunctions to well-known persons against the use of their name, image or voice in AI-generated content without consent.","q":[{"q":"Under Section 2(d)(vi) of the Copyright Act as described, the author of a computer-generated work is","o":["the computer","the programmer of the software in all cases","no one, as the work is not protected","the person who causes the work to be created"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states this directly.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The standard of originality adopted in Eastern Book Company v. D.B. Modak is","o":["a modicum of creativity involving intellectual effort and skill","sweat of the brow","creative genius","novelty and non-obviousness"],"a":0,"e":"The Court adopted the modicum of creativity standard.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following is the legal question raised in the ANI v. OpenAI litigation, as described in the passage?","o":["Whether news can be copyrighted at all","Whether OpenAI may register a trademark","Whether a large language model can hold a patent","Whether the use of copyrighted works to train an AI model and the reproduction of such works in outputs infringes copyright, having regard to the closed list of exceptions in Section 52"],"a":3,"e":"The passage describes the allegations and the issue of the Section 52 exceptions.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which statement is correct about patentability of computer-related inventions?","o":["Business methods are always patentable","A computer programme per se is excluded, but one showing a technical effect or contribution may be patentable","All inventions involving software are excluded by Section 3(k)","Software is always patentable"],"a":1,"e":"Ferid Allani held that technical effect inventions are not excluded merely because they involve a programme.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The list of exceptions in Section 52 is described in the passage as 'closed'. The consequence is that","o":["there are no exceptions in Indian law","all uses of copyrighted works are exempt","courts may add new exceptions freely, such as AI training","a use not falling within the listed exceptions cannot claim exemption under Section 52, though a court may construe the listed heads"],"a":3,"e":"A closed list restricts exemptions to those enumerated, leaving interpretation of the listed heads to courts.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A person's voice is cloned by an AI tool and used in a commercial advertisement without his consent. The most suitable legal route indicated in the passage is","o":["a patent infringement action","a personality-rights action for an injunction","no remedy is available","a challenge to the Copyright Act"],"a":1,"e":"The passage notes that High Courts have granted personality-rights injunctions in such cases.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P13","s":"Family Law","sf":"Family Law","t":"Divorce, Maintenance and the Move Towards Uniformity","x":"Section 13B of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 allows divorce by mutual consent on a joint petition after the parties have lived separately for a year, with a further period of six months between the first motion and the second motion. In Amardeep Singh v. Harveen Kaur (2017), the Supreme Court held that the six-month period is directory and may be waived by the court in appropriate cases. In Shilpa Sailesh v. Varun Sreenivasan (2023), a Constitution Bench held that the Court may, in exercise of its power under Article 142, dissolve a marriage on the ground of irretrievable breakdown, even though this is not a ground for divorce under the Act, where the marriage is wholly unworkable, emotionally dead and beyond salvage, but that the power should be exercised with care and not as a matter of right; the Bench also laid down factors such as the duration of the marriage, the nature of allegations and the attempts at settlement. In Rajnesh v. Neha (2020), the Court issued guidelines on maintenance, including the filing of an affidavit of disclosure of assets by both parties, and held that maintenance should be awarded from the date of the application. In Mohd. Abdul Samad v. State of Telangana (2024), the Court held that a divorced Muslim woman can claim maintenance under Section 125 of the Code of Criminal Procedure (now Section 144 of the BNSS), irrespective of her personal law, and that the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986 does not displace that remedy. The State of Uttarakhand enacted the Uniform Civil Code, 2024, which came into force in January 2025, requiring the registration of marriages and live-in relationships; critics argue that the State lacks exclusive competence, since personal law falls in Entry 5 of the Concurrent List, while supporters rely on Article 44 of the Constitution.","q":[{"q":"The effect of Amardeep Singh v. Harveen Kaur on the six-month period under Section 13B is that it is","o":["mandatory and cannot be waived","directory, and may be waived in appropriate cases","applicable only to Muslims","abolished altogether"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the period is directory and waivable.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Irretrievable breakdown of marriage, in the passage, is","o":["not a statutory ground, but the Supreme Court may dissolve a marriage on this basis under Article 142, with care","a ground only before family courts","a ground available to every spouse as of right","a statutory ground for divorce under the Hindu Marriage Act"],"a":0,"e":"Shilpa Sailesh permits the use of Article 142 and cautions against treating it as a right.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to Rajnesh v. Neha, which of the following is correct?","o":["Maintenance is never payable before the final order","Both parties must file an affidavit of disclosure of assets, and maintenance is awarded from the date of the application","Maintenance is capped by statute","Only the husband files the affidavit"],"a":1,"e":"The passage records both directions.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A divorced Muslim woman applies for maintenance under Section 125 CrPC. On the basis of Samad, her claim is","o":["maintainable only under the 1986 Act","barred by personal law","maintainable only if she has no children","maintainable irrespective of her personal law"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that Section 125 is available irrespective of personal law.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The principal ground of criticism of the Uttarakhand UCC in the passage is that","o":["it violates Article 44","the State lacks exclusive competence as personal law is on the Concurrent List","it applies to all of India","it does not provide for any registration"],"a":1,"e":"The passage records this as the critics' argument.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best summary of the trend in the passage?","o":["Courts have used flexible interpretive and constitutional tools to prevent hardship in matrimonial disputes, while legislatures consider uniformity","Courts have rigidly applied statutory waiting periods","Courts have prohibited maintenance","Parliament has abolished divorce"],"a":0,"e":"The passage shows judicial flexibility and legislative debate on uniformity.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P14","s":"Criminal Law","sf":"Criminal Law / Constitutional Law","t":"Remission, Life Imprisonment and the Appropriate Government","x":"Articles 72 and 161 of the Constitution empower the President and the Governor to grant pardons, reprieves, respites or remissions, and to suspend, remit or commute sentences. Statutory remission is regulated by Section 432 of the Code of Criminal Procedure (now Section 473 of the BNSS), under which the 'appropriate Government' may remit the sentence, subject to Section 433A, which requires a person sentenced to life for an offence punishable with death to serve at least fourteen years. In Union of India v. V. Sriharan (2016), a Constitution Bench held that life imprisonment means imprisonment for the remainder of the convict's natural life, subject to remission, and that the Supreme Court and High Courts may impose a fixed term of more than fourteen years, or a term without remission, as a substitute for the death penalty. In the Bilkis Bano case (Bilkis Yakub Rasool v. Union of India, January 2024), the Supreme Court quashed the orders of the Gujarat Government remitting the sentences of eleven convicts who had been sentenced to life for gang rape and murder during the 2002 riots. It held that the 'appropriate Government' for the purpose of remission is the State within whose territory the convict was sentenced, not the State where the offence occurred, and that, since the trial had been transferred to Maharashtra by the Supreme Court, the State of Maharashtra alone could have considered remission. The Court also held that an earlier order in the case directing Gujarat to consider remission had been obtained by suppression of material facts and was a fraud on the Court, and that the discretion to grant remission is subject to judicial review and must be exercised on relevant considerations and in a non-arbitrary manner. In Shatrughan Chauhan v. Union of India (2014), the Court held that an unexplained and inordinate delay in the disposal of a mercy petition is a ground for commutation of the death sentence.","q":[{"q":"According to V. Sriharan, life imprisonment means","o":["imprisonment which is automatically commuted after fourteen years","imprisonment for the remainder of the natural life of the convict, subject to remission","twenty years' imprisonment","fourteen years' imprisonment"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states this principle.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Bilkis Bano, the Court held that the appropriate Government for remission is","o":["the State in which the offence occurred","the Union Government","the State within whose territory the convict was sentenced","the State of the victim's residence"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that it is the State where the trial took place and sentence was passed; here, Maharashtra.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The reason the Court gave for quashing the earlier order directing Gujarat to consider remission was that it","o":["was contrary to Article 161","was passed after fourteen years","was passed by the wrong Bench","was obtained by suppression of material facts and amounted to a fraud on the Court"],"a":3,"e":"The passage records the finding of suppression and fraud.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is a ground for commutation of a death sentence as described in the passage?","o":["The offender's good behaviour in the prison","Unexplained and inordinate delay in the disposal of a mercy petition","The conviction by a High Court","The age of the victim"],"a":1,"e":"Shatrughan Chauhan treats such delay as a ground for commutation.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A court wishes to substitute the death penalty with a sentence of thirty years without remission. According to the passage,","o":["only the legislature may do so","only the President may do so","it lacks power to do so","the Supreme Court and High Courts may impose a fixed term exceeding fourteen years or without remission as an alternative to death"],"a":3,"e":"Sriharan permits constitutional courts to impose such sentences.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which proposition best reflects the effect of the Bilkis Bano decision on remission powers?","o":["Remission cannot be granted in cases of rape","Remission is an unreviewable executive favour","Remission can be granted only by the President","Remission is a discretion subject to judicial review and must be exercised by the competent Government on relevant considerations"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held remission to be reviewable and tied to the appropriate Government.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P15","s":"Labour Law","sf":"Labour Law","t":"The Four Labour Codes","x":"On 21 November 2025, the Union Government brought into force the four Labour Codes, namely the Code on Wages, 2019, the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, the Code on Social Security, 2020 and the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020, consolidating twenty-nine central labour laws. The Code on Wages provides for a statutory floor wage, universalises the entitlement to minimum wages and timely payment, and defines 'wages' so that, in general, remuneration components other than specified exclusions must not exceed fifty per cent of the total remuneration, which expands the wage base for calculation of benefits. The Industrial Relations Code raises the threshold for prior government permission for lay-off, retrenchment and closure from one hundred to three hundred workers, requires a notice of fourteen days for a strike in all establishments, and recognises 'fixed-term employment' on terms equal to permanent workers, with eligibility for gratuity after one year of service rather than five. The Code on Social Security for the first time defines 'gig worker' and 'platform worker' and requires aggregators to contribute a percentage of annual turnover, subject to a ceiling on the payments made to such workers, to a social security fund. The Occupational Safety Code permits women to work night shifts with their consent and with safety measures, and requires employers to offer free annual health check-ups to workers above a specified age. Trade unions have criticised the Codes for easing retrenchment and restricting strikes, while the Government states that they would formalise employment, simplify compliance and extend social security.","q":[{"q":"The threshold for prior government permission for retrenchment under the Industrial Relations Code is","o":["three hundred workers","two hundred workers","one hundred workers","five hundred workers"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that it is raised from 100 to 300.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A fixed-term employee completes one year of service. Under the Codes, as described, he is eligible for","o":["pension","no benefit until five years","gratuity","only wages"],"a":2,"e":"Fixed-term employees are eligible for gratuity after one year.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following correctly reflects the rule regarding 'wages' in the Code on Wages?","o":["Excluded components generally must not exceed fifty per cent of the total remuneration, thereby widening the wage base","Wages exclude all allowances","Allowances may form any proportion of total remuneration","Basic pay must be at least ninety per cent of remuneration"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the fifty per cent rule.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Code on Social Security, in relation to gig and platform workers, provides that","o":["they are excluded from all benefits","only the Government contributes","workers contribute the whole amount","aggregators contribute a percentage of annual turnover, subject to a cap linked to payments to such workers, to a fund"],"a":3,"e":"The passage describes the aggregator contribution subject to a ceiling.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT stated as a feature of the Codes in the passage?","o":["Night shifts for women with consent","Free annual health check-up above a specified age","Abolition of trade unions","Fourteen days' notice for strike in all establishments"],"a":2,"e":"Abolition of unions is not mentioned; criticism is that strikes are restricted.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The main criticism of trade unions about the Codes, as per the passage, is that they","o":["apply only to government employees","ease retrenchment and restrict strikes","abolish gratuity","increase minimum wages excessively"],"a":1,"e":"The passage records this criticism.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P16","s":"Insolvency and Company Law","sf":"Insolvency and Company Law","t":"The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code: Creditors, Clean Slate and Commercial Wisdom","x":"The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 (IBC) provides for a time-bound resolution of the insolvency of corporate debtors. A financial creditor may initiate the corporate insolvency resolution process (CIRP) under Section 7, an operational creditor under Section 9, and the corporate debtor itself under Section 10. On admission, a moratorium under Section 14 prohibits the institution or continuation of suits and the enforcement of security against the debtor. An interim resolution professional takes control, and a committee of creditors (CoC), comprising financial creditors, is constituted. Section 29A bars certain persons, including wilful defaulters and persons whose accounts have been classified as non-performing assets for a specified period, from submitting a resolution plan; the Supreme Court in Swiss Ribbons v. Union of India (2019) upheld the constitutional validity of the Code, and in ArcelorMittal India v. Satish Kumar Gupta (2019) held that Section 29A applies to the resolution applicant and persons connected with him. In K. Sashidhar v. Indian Overseas Bank (2019), the Court held that the commercial wisdom of the CoC is non-justiciable and that the adjudicating authority cannot interfere with the merits of the decision to approve a resolution plan, though it may examine whether the plan complies with the statutory requirements. In Committee of Creditors of Essar Steel v. Satish Kumar Gupta (2019), the Court held that the CoC may differentiate between classes of creditors, but that the equitable treatment of similarly situated creditors is to be observed. In Ghanashyam Mishra and Sons v. Edelweiss Asset Reconstruction (2021), the Court held that once a resolution plan is approved by the adjudicating authority, all claims not part of the plan stand extinguished, giving the successful applicant a 'clean slate', so that it can start without undecided claims. Where no plan is approved, the corporate debtor goes into liquidation, in which the proceeds are distributed under the waterfall in Section 53.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following may initiate CIRP under Section 9 of the Code?","o":["The corporate debtor","A shareholder","A financial creditor","An operational creditor"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that an operational creditor initiates under Section 9.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The principal effect of the moratorium under Section 14 is that","o":["the debtor must pay all debts at once","the debtor's assets vest in the Government","the CoC is dissolved","suits and enforcement of security against the debtor are barred during CIRP"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states this effect.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"According to K. Sashidhar, the adjudicating authority","o":["cannot interfere with the commercial wisdom of the CoC, though it may check statutory compliance","may substitute its own view for the commercial wisdom of the CoC","must approve every plan","has no role in approving a resolution plan"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held commercial wisdom to be non-justiciable subject to statutory compliance review.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The 'clean slate' principle in Ghanashyam Mishra means that","o":["the resolution applicant is liable for all past debts","the creditors may file fresh claims at any time","claims not part of the approved plan stand extinguished","the plan is subject to renewal each year"],"a":2,"e":"This is the principle as described in the passage.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A company's promoter, a wilful defaulter, proposes a resolution plan through a related entity. Under the passage, the plan is","o":["permissible if the plan pays all creditors in full","permissible only with the approval of the CoC","barred, as Section 29A applies to the applicant and persons connected with him","permissible, since he is not the applicant"],"a":2,"e":"ArcelorMittal held that Section 29A extends to connected persons.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Which of the following is a correct inference about the CoC's power to differentiate between creditors, from Essar Steel?","o":["The CoC has no power over distribution","The CoC may differentiate between classes of creditors, but must ensure the equitable treatment of similarly situated creditors","The CoC has unrestricted power to discriminate","The CoC must treat all creditors identically"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the qualified power.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P17","s":"Law of Evidence","sf":"Law of Evidence","t":"Confessions, Discovery, Dying Declarations and Circumstantial Evidence","x":"A confession made to a police officer is inadmissible against the accused under the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 (and the corresponding provision of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023), and a confession made while in police custody is inadmissible unless made in the immediate presence of a Magistrate. This protection arises from the fear that confessions to the police may be extracted by coercion. The proviso, Section 27, provides that so much of the information received from the accused in custody, whether it amounts to a confession or not, as relates distinctly to the fact thereby discovered, may be proved. In Pulukuri Kottaya v. King Emperor (1947), the Privy Council explained that the 'fact discovered' embraces the place from which the object is produced and the knowledge of the accused as to this, and that only the information distinctly connected with the discovery is admissible, not the whole statement. A dying declaration is admissible under Section 32(1) as a statement made by a person as to the cause of his death or the circumstances of the transaction resulting in his death, and in Laxman v. State of Maharashtra (2002), a Constitution Bench held that a dying declaration is not invalid merely because it was not recorded by a Magistrate or lacks a certificate of the doctor as to fitness of mind, though the court must be satisfied that it is voluntary and truthful. Under Section 101, the burden of proving a fact lies on the person who asserts it, but Section 106 provides that where a fact is especially within the knowledge of a person, the burden of proving it lies on him; this does not relieve the prosecution of its primary burden, as the Supreme Court has stressed. In Sharad Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra (1984), the Court laid down that in a case of circumstantial evidence, the circumstances must be fully established, be consistent only with the guilt of the accused and form a chain so complete that no reasonable ground for the conclusion consistent with his innocence remains.","q":[{"q":"An accused in police custody tells the Investigating Officer, 'I killed him and hid the knife under the bridge'. The knife is recovered from under the bridge. Which part is admissible under Section 27?","o":["The entire statement","Only the part that he killed the deceased","None of it","Only the part that relates distinctly to the discovery, i.e. the place of concealment of the knife"],"a":3,"e":"Only the portion distinctly relating to the fact discovered is admissible; the confession of the killing is inadmissible.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to Laxman v. State of Maharashtra, a dying declaration","o":["is invalid unless recorded by a Magistrate","requires corroboration in every case","is not invalid merely because it was not recorded by a Magistrate or lacks a medical certificate, if the court is satisfied that it is voluntary and truthful","is invalid if the declarant survives"],"a":2,"e":"This is the holding stated in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Section 106 of the Evidence Act, as described, means that","o":["the burden of proving a fact especially within his knowledge lies on the person, but the prosecution's primary burden remains","the prosecution is relieved of proof in all cases","the court must presume guilt","the accused must prove his innocence"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that Section 106 does not relieve the prosecution of the primary burden.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a requirement of the chain of circumstances in Sharad Sarda?","o":["The circumstances must be fully established","They must be consistent only with the hypothesis of guilt","There must be an eye-witness to the crime","They must form a chain so complete that no reasonable ground for innocence remains"],"a":2,"e":"Circumstantial evidence applies where there is no eye-witness; the rest are requirements.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A confession made by an accused in police custody before a police constable, and not in the presence of a Magistrate, is","o":["admissible if recorded on video","admissible if voluntary","inadmissible against the accused","admissible if corroborated"],"a":2,"e":"Under the passage, such confession is inadmissible unless made in the presence of a Magistrate.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following best explains the rationale for the exclusion of confessions to the police?","o":["The accused has no right to speak","The fear that confessions may be extracted by coercion","Confessions are never relevant","Police officers are unreliable witnesses in all cases"],"a":1,"e":"The passage identifies the fear of coercion as the rationale.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P18","s":"Property Law","sf":"Property Law","t":"Transfer of Immovable Property: Form, Possession and Notice","x":"Under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a sale of tangible immovable property of the value of one hundred rupees and upwards can be made only by a registered instrument; the mere delivery of possession or the payment of the price does not transfer ownership, and a contract for sale does not itself create any interest in or charge on the property. Section 53A, however, protects a transferee who has taken possession in part performance of a written contract signed by the transferor, where the transferee has done some act in furtherance of the contract and is willing to perform his part; the transferor is then debarred from enforcing against him any right in respect of the property other than those expressly provided in the contract. The protection is, however, available only as a shield by way of defence, and not as a sword to claim title. In Suraj Lamp and Industries v. State of Haryana (2011), the Supreme Court held that transactions styled as sale by a General Power of Attorney, agreement to sell and will do not convey title, and cannot be recognised as completed transfers, since a sale must be by a registered deed of conveyance, although the Court clarified that its decision would not affect genuine agency arrangements and earlier transactions already acted upon. Section 52 embodies the doctrine of lis pendens: during the pendency of a suit in which the right to immovable property is directly and specifically in question, the property cannot be transferred so as to affect the rights of any other party, so that a transferee pendente lite is bound by the result of the suit even without notice. Section 41 protects a transferee from an ostensible owner who has acted with the consent of the real owner, where the transferee has taken reasonable care and acted in good faith.","q":[{"q":"A buys a house from B for Rs 50 lakh, pays the entire price and takes possession, but no deed is registered. Under the passage, ownership","o":["passed on the date of the agreement","has passed to A by delivery of possession","passed upon payment of the price","has not passed, since a sale of such property requires a registered instrument"],"a":3,"e":"Section 54 requires a registered instrument; possession and payment alone do not pass title.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Section 53A can be invoked by","o":["a transferor against a third person","a transferee in possession under a written contract, as a defence against the transferor","any person in possession of the property","a transferee, as a ground to claim title in a suit"],"a":1,"e":"The doctrine operates as a shield and not as a sword.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The holding in Suraj Lamp is that","o":["all GPAs are void","a GPA sale conveys good title","GPA, agreement to sell and will arrangements do not amount to a completed transfer of title","sales need not be registered"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that such transactions do not pass title.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"During the pendency of a suit about ownership of a plot, the defendant sells the plot to C, who has no notice of the suit. On the basis of Section 52, C","o":["takes free of the suit","is bound by the result of the suit, even without notice","is entitled to ask for refund only","must be joined, or the suit abates"],"a":1,"e":"The doctrine of lis pendens binds the transferee pendente lite irrespective of notice.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is a requirement for protection under Section 41?","o":["The real owner must be a company","The transferor must be a minor","The transferee must have taken reasonable care and acted in good faith, and the ostensible owner must have acted with the real owner's consent","The transfer must be gratuitous"],"a":2,"e":"The passage lists these requirements.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is the most accurate?","o":["Registration is optional in all sales","Part performance confers on the transferee a defensive right against the transferor, but title still requires a registered conveyance","Lis pendens applies only if notice is served","Part performance gives a transferee a title as against the world"],"a":1,"e":"Combining Sections 54 and 53A shows that part performance is defensive and does not substitute for a registered sale deed.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P19","s":"Administrative Law","sf":"Administrative Law","t":"Judicial Review: Reasonableness, Proportionality, Legitimate Expectation and Tribunals","x":"Judicial review of administrative action in India rests on the tests of illegality, irrationality and procedural impropriety. The Wednesbury test, from Associated Provincial Picture Houses v. Wednesbury Corporation (1948), treats a decision as unreasonable only where it is so outrageous in its defiance of logic that no sensible person could have arrived at it. Indian courts have also developed the doctrine of proportionality, which asks whether the measure is suitable, necessary and balanced in its effect on the right affected. In Om Kumar v. Union of India (2001), the Supreme Court explained that where an administrative action affects fundamental rights, the court examines the proportionality of the action as primary reviewer, whereas in the case of other rights Wednesbury review applies, with the court confined to the decision-making process. The doctrine of legitimate expectation protects a person who has been led by a promise or a settled practice to expect a benefit; in Navjyoti Co-operative Group Housing Society v. Union of India (1992), the Court held that the expectation may be defeated only for a good public reason and after a fair hearing; it does not give a vested right. The principles of natural justice, nemo judex in causa sua and audi alteram partem, have been given an Article 14 and Article 21 basis in Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978), so that procedure affecting rights must be fair, just and reasonable. In L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997), the Supreme Court held that tribunals, though they can decide questions of law and fact, remain subject to the writ jurisdiction of the High Courts under Articles 226 and 227, since judicial review is part of the basic structure. In Madras Bar Association v. Union of India (2021), the Court struck down provisions of the Tribunals Reforms Act, 2021 prescribing a minimum age of fifty years and a four-year tenure for tribunal members, holding that they undermined judicial independence.","q":[{"q":"According to Om Kumar, when administrative action affects fundamental rights, the court","o":["declines to interfere","applies Wednesbury review only","applies the test of legitimate expectation only","examines proportionality as primary reviewer"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that proportionality is applied by the court as primary reviewer in such cases.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Wednesbury test of unreasonableness is satisfied where","o":["the decision was made by a junior officer","the decision causes loss to a citizen","the decision is merely harsh","the decision is so outrageous in its defiance of logic that no sensible person could have reached it"],"a":3,"e":"This is the standard stated in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A private builder is told by a municipal body, following a consistent policy for ten years, that plot allotments are made to registered cooperatives. The policy is changed suddenly without a hearing. On the principle of legitimate expectation in the passage,","o":["the expectation may be defeated only for good public reason and after a fair hearing","the expectation gives a right to damages","there is a vested right to the allotment","legitimate expectation is not recognised in India"],"a":0,"e":"Navjyoti states that the expectation is not a vested right but can be defeated only for a good public reason with a fair hearing.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"After Maneka Gandhi, the requirements of natural justice are traced to","o":["Article 14 and Article 21","Article 32 only","the Directive Principles","Article 311"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that natural justice has an Article 14 and Article 21 basis.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In L. Chandra Kumar, the Court held that tribunals","o":["remain subject to writ jurisdiction of High Courts under Articles 226 and 227","are outside the purview of High Court review","can only decide questions of fact","must follow only the executive's directions"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held so because judicial review is part of the basic structure.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best reason for the decision in Madras Bar Association (2021) in striking down the provisions on age and tenure?","o":["The provisions created new tribunals","The provisions undermined the independence of the judiciary","The provisions applied only to High Courts","The provisions violated federalism"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that they undermined judicial independence.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P20","s":"Public International Law","sf":"Public International Law","t":"Treaties, Suspension and the Indus Waters Treaty","x":"The law of treaties is codified in the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969 (VCLT), many of whose rules are regarded as reflecting customary international law. Article 26 embodies the principle of pacta sunt servanda: every treaty in force is binding on the parties and must be performed in good faith. A party may not invoke its internal law as justification for failure to perform (Article 27). Article 56 provides that a treaty which contains no provision regarding termination or denunciation is not subject to denunciation or withdrawal, unless it is established that the parties intended to admit the possibility of denunciation, or such a right is implied by the nature of the treaty. Article 60 allows a party specially affected by a material breach by another party to invoke the breach as a ground for terminating or suspending the treaty, and Article 62 permits termination only for a fundamental change of circumstances that was not foreseen and which radically transforms the obligations still to be performed; the ICJ in Gabcikovo-Nagymaros Project (Hungary/Slovakia) (1997) stressed that these grounds must be applied restrictively, in the interest of the stability of treaty relations. Article 65 prescribes a procedure of notice and, in case of objection, peaceful settlement of disputes. The Indus Waters Treaty of 1960 between India and Pakistan contains no clause for unilateral termination; it provides for a Permanent Indus Commission and a graded dispute resolution mechanism comprising questions, differences and disputes, which may lead to a Neutral Expert or a Court of Arbitration. In April 2025, following a terrorist attack in Pahalgam, India announced that the treaty would be held 'in abeyance' until Pakistan credibly and irrevocably abjures cross-border terrorism, while Pakistan described any interference with water flows as an act of war. A Court of Arbitration constituted under the treaty issued awards in 2025 asserting its competence notwithstanding the announcement, which India rejects as the proceedings in its view are illegally constituted. India is not a party to the VCLT, though it accepts many of its rules as customary.","q":[{"q":"The principle of pacta sunt servanda means that","o":["treaties bind only upon ratification by the UN","every treaty in force binds the parties and must be performed in good faith","treaties may be terminated at will","domestic law prevails over treaties"],"a":1,"e":"Article 26 as set out in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Under Article 56 of the VCLT, as described, a treaty without a termination clause","o":["terminates after fifty years","is not subject to denunciation or withdrawal unless the parties intended such a possibility or it is implied by the nature of the treaty","is void","may always be denounced unilaterally"],"a":1,"e":"This is the rule stated in the passage and is relevant to the Indus Waters Treaty.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best statement of the ICJ's attitude in Gabcikovo-Nagymaros, as per the passage?","o":["The grounds of termination or suspension must be applied restrictively to preserve the stability of treaty relations","Material breach and fundamental change are easily invoked","States may suspend treaties if their internal law so provides","The VCLT does not apply to river treaties"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states this directly.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A State claims it may suspend a treaty because of a terrorist attack attributed to the other party, without invoking Articles 60, 62 or 65 or any treaty clause. On the basis of the passage, the strongest objection is that","o":["the State lacks sovereignty","the treaty must be performed only by the weaker State","the VCLT grounds are limited, require specific conditions and a procedure, and Article 27 bars reliance on internal law, so a bare unilateral 'abeyance' lacks an evident basis","terrorism is irrelevant in international law"],"a":2,"e":"The VCLT confines termination or suspension to specified grounds (e.g. Articles 60 and 62), prescribes a procedure (Article 65), and Article 27 bars reliance on internal law. A bare unilateral 'abeyance' without a treaty clause or recognised ground lacks an evident legal basis. (The State may counter that the attack is a material breach, but that is an argument under Article 60, not a ground outside it.)","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The dispute resolution mechanism under the Indus Waters Treaty, per the passage, comprises","o":["a graded mechanism including questions, differences and disputes, leading to a Neutral Expert or a Court of Arbitration","only bilateral negotiation","only the ICJ","only the Permanent Indus Commission"],"a":0,"e":"The passage describes the graded structure.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is correct on the basis of the passage?","o":["The Court of Arbitration was established by the UN Security Council","Pakistan is not bound by the treaty","India is not a party to the VCLT, though it treats many rules as customary","India is a party to the VCLT"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Easy"}]}]};
